        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Ancient Near East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of the Granicus</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">May 334 BCE, in the Macedonian month of Daisios</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The crossing of the Granicus (now the Biga River) in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troad">Troad</a>, on the road from Abydus to Dascylium, in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>; the exact ford is debated</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> under Alexander III, with 12,000 heavy infantry, 1,000 light infantry and 5,100 cavalry, 18,100 men in all; the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, with Arsites probably in overall command, and Memnon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, with strengths that range from 14,000 or 15,000 in one modern estimate to almost 20,000 infantry and 20,000 cavalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Alexander defeated the Persian field army and the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> behind it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> were forced onto the defensive in the cities they still held in the region.</span></div>
        </div>
