    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Strengths for the Persian army run from 14,000 or 15,000 in one modern estimate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>'s almost 20,000 infantry and 20,000 cavalry, and Justin's 600,000 is judged fantastical.</li><li>The ancient accounts disagree about how the battle began: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> describe an assault across a defended river, Diodorus a crossing at dawn that met no resistance.</li><li>The Persian army was not destroyed, and many of its men retreated to Halicarnassus, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> fell and Alexander marched on to Halicarnassus without significant obstacles.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Before the battle some Macedonian officers objected that custom forbade fighting in the month of Daisios, and Alexander is said to have solved this by declaring the month a second Artemision.</p></aside>
    </section>

