    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">338 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Philip II wins at Chaeronea and forms the Hellenic League to make war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> is murdered</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">336 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Macedonian expeditionary corps lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, Philip is murdered and Alexander III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> take their thrones</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">335 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Memnon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> defeats a Macedonian force and most Greek cities return to Persian control</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Spring 334 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander marches to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> and crosses into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 334 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander defeats the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>' army at the Granicus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">334 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Dascylium, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> fall to Alexander or are evacuated by their garrisons</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">334 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander besieges Miletus, which resists him</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-granicus" data-v="d8a4713d92" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Both armies drawn up on either side of the river</b> The Persian cavalry stood along the east bank with the Greek mercenaries behind it. Alexander took the right wing of the Macedonian line and Parmenion the left, and the Persians moved cavalry to their own left when they saw Alexander opposite it.</li><li><b>Amyntas attacks the Persian left wing</b> After a moment of silence Alexander ordered Amyntas to attack the Persian left wing, with the squadron of Socrates at the front. The Persians answered the charge with volleys of javelins.</li><li><b>Amyntas's force is pushed back</b> Outnumbered, and facing Persians on higher ground at the top of the bank, the vanguard suffered losses and retreated towards Alexander. Alexander now attacked with the remaining Companion cavalry of the right wing.</li><li><b>Alexander crosses and fights on the far bank</b> Alexander and his men engaged the Persians and their leaders in a hard-fought fight. Other Macedonian cavalry established a foothold on the bank and reinforced his force.</li><li><b>The Persian cavalry is driven back and breaks</b> The Macedonian cavalry and the light infantry among them forced the Persian cavalry back, first on the left where Alexander was fighting. Then the centre broke and the Persian cavalry on both wings fled.</li><li><b>The Greek mercenaries are attacked from all sides</b> Alexander did not pursue the fleeing cavalry far. The mercenaries, mostly inactive at their original position, were surprised and attacked by the Foot Companions from the front and the cavalry from all other directions. They were soon defeated and only a few escaped.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-iii.jpg" alt="Darius III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_III_mosaic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After winning the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> forced most of the Greek states into a military alliance, the Hellenic League. Its goal was to make war on the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> to avenge the second Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> in 480 BCE. The empire had been in crisis since the murder of its king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a>, in 338 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> again seceded. In the spring of 336 BCE Philip ordered a Macedonian expeditionary corps of several thousand soldiers to land on the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, most likely under Parmenion, with Attalus as second in command. Many Greek cities there surrendered peacefully.</p><p>At the end of 336 BCE this changed. Philip was murdered, most likely in October, and his son Alexander III first had to put down revolts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> had become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a> around the autumn of 336 BCE, stabilised the empire and started a counteroffensive. After the Egyptian revolt was suppressed, he sent Memnon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> to Asia Minor with a force of Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. Memnon defeated a Macedonian force at either <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Magnesia ad Sipylum</a> or Magnesia on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a>, probably in early spring 335 BCE. Later that year another Macedonian force was defeated by a Persian army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troad">Troad</a>. By the end of 335 BCE most of the Greek cities were again under Persian control, and the expeditionary force held only Abydus and perhaps Rhoeteum.</p><p>Alexander marched his army to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> in early spring 334 BCE. Darius had probably been informed of his movements for some time, but left the defence of Asia Minor to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/arrian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arrian.jpg" alt="Arrian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrian7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WikiSysop, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alexander's army that crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> numbered around 32,000 infantry and around 5,000 cavalry, but the Greek allied and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> infantry were not used in the battle. The force that fought had 12,000 heavy infantry, including the Foot Companions and the elite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypaspists">hypaspists</a>, 1,000 light infantry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and Agrianian javelin-men, and 5,100 cavalry, which makes 18,100 men. Alexander commanded the right wing, where he placed seven of his eight squadrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a>. Parmenion commanded the left, with the Thracian, allied Greek and Thessalian cavalry.</p><p>For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> gives almost 20,000 infantry, all Greek mercenaries, and 20,000 cavalry. Diodorus gives 10,000 cavalry and 100,000 infantry, and Justin 600,000 men in all. Azar Gat holds that Greek sources wildly exaggerated the size of Persian armies. N. G. L. Hammond accepts Arrian's numbers, while A. M. Devine and Krzysztof Nawotka call Justin's and Diodorus's infantry figures fantastical. Devine suggests an army of 14,000 to 15,000, with 4,000 or 5,000 Greek mercenaries, and Nawotka and Ernst Badian think part of the infantry was local levies.</p><p>The army was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> and commanders including Arsites, Spithridates, Arsames, Rheomithres and Memnon, with Arsites probably in overall command. At a council at Zeleia, Arrian and Diodorus say Memnon advised a scorched earth strategy, but the others refused. The Persians placed their cavalry along the river bank, and the Greek mercenaries, led by Omares, stood behind them. When they saw Alexander opposite their left wing, they moved cavalry there.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Granicus&#8221; cites where it covers Persian army:</p><ul><li>Azar Gat, War in Human Civilization (Oxford University Press, 2006)</li><li>J. F. C. Fuller, The generalship of Alexander the Great (Rutgers University Press, 1960)</li><li>Krzysztof Nawotka, Alexander the Great (Cambridge Scholars Publishing, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> reports that when Alexander learned where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> stood, he formed his army for battle. Parmenion advised camping on the west bank and crossing at dawn, but Alexander ordered an immediate attack. Alexander sent Amyntas, son of Arrhabaeus, against the Persian left wing, with the Companion squadron of Socrates, now led by Ptolemy, son of Philip, in front. The Persians answered with javelin volleys. Amyntas's force, outnumbered and below the Persians on the bank, suffered losses and fell back towards Alexander, who then attacked with the rest of the right-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a>.</p><p>Devine reads the first attack as a ruse to draw the Persian cavalry out of formation into the riverbed. How Alexander crossed is disputed. Fuller and Bosworth have him moving diagonally downstream towards the Persian centre. Badian has him attacking the massed left wing, and Hammond has him moving upstream to keep his line as it crossed.</p><p>In the hard fighting the Macedonian lance proved more effective than the Persian javelin. Alexander broke his lance, and Demaratus of Corinth gave him another. He killed Mithridates, son-in-law of Darius. Cleitus the Black cut off the sword arm of Spithridates as he was about to strike Alexander from behind. The Persian left and then the centre were broken, and the Persian cavalry on both wings fled. Alexander did not pursue far but turned on the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, who had mostly stayed at their position. They were attacked by the Foot Companions from the front and the cavalry from all other directions, and only a few escaped.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Granicus&#8221; cites where it covers Account of Arrian, Account of Plutarch, Modern interpretations:</p><ul><li>Peter Green, Alexander of Macedon, 356-323 B.C.: A Historical Biography (University of California Press, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Macedonian losses, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, were 25 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a> in the first attack, 60 other cavalry and 30 infantry, 115 killed in all. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> gives 34 dead and Justin 9 infantry and 120 cavalry. On the Persian side many leaders died, among them Niphates, Petenes, Mithrobuzanes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of Cappadocia, Arbupales, Pharnaces and Omares. Arsites fled into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> and later committed suicide, blamed for the defeat. Arrian states that 1,000 Persian cavalry were killed and 2,000 Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> taken prisoner. Plutarch gives 2,500 cavalry and 20,000 infantry killed, and Diodorus 2,000 cavalry and 10,000 infantry killed and 20,000 prisoners. Devine, who judges the last two impossibly large, puts the total Persian loss at 5,000 to 6,000.</p><p>The 2,000 captured mercenaries were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> to work the land as slaves, because Alexander held that they had betrayed fellow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and broken the Hellenic League's agreement. He sent 300 suits of Persian armour to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> as an offering to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>. Dascylium was evacuated and occupied by Parmenion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> surrendered, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> was evacuated by its garrison of Greek mercenaries. Magnesia and Tralles offered surrender. Alexander sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> to the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> to break up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchies</a> and reinstate democratic government. Miletus resisted, and he besieged it.</p><p>A large part of the Persian army retreated to Halicarnassus.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Granicus&#8221; cites where it covers Macedonian army, Persian army, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Plutarch, Lives (Harvard University Press, 1919)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus.jpg" alt="Diodorus Siculus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diodoro_siculo_-_storico_di_Agira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Three ancient accounts survive, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>. Arrian's is the longest and most detailed. Plutarch's is shorter, concentrates on Alexander's combats with the Persian leaders, and agrees with Arrian except in small points: it puts Persian infantry as well as cavalry in the front line and has the Foot Companions cross the river while the cavalry fought. Diodorus differs fundamentally: the armies camp on opposite banks, and Alexander crosses unopposed at dawn the next day. Most historians dismiss it, since a night or dawn crossing would probably have been detected and opposed, and Diodorus's separate cavalry and infantry battle is uncharacteristic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a>.</p><p>Devine notes that Arrian's casualty figures come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a>, who drew on Callisthenes, and that Plutarch's come from Aristobulus. Arrian's order of events may have made Alexander look more heroic, and the debates with Parmenion may come from Callisthenes. Peter Green, in 1974, tried to reconcile Diodorus and Arrian by supposing a failed first crossing against infantry that Alexander's propagandists later covered up, but in 2013 he conceded that the theory was not convincing.</p><p>The site is also disputed. The ford has traditionally been placed above the junction of the Gümüşçay river and the Biga River, or further downstream. Hammond placed it between Gümüşçay and Çeşmealtı, where there was a ridge for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and perhaps an older riverbed. Modern observers find the river easy to cross, against the ancient writers' emphasis on hardship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lysippus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lysippus.jpg" alt="Lysippus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lysippus">Lysippus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_base_with_signature_of_Lysippos,_Corinth_(inv._I_29;_Corinth_VIII.1,_34).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mark Landon, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle was the first of three major battles between Alexander and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. The Persian military losses were not disastrous, but the strategic outcome was precarious for the empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satraps</a> had been defeated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> before and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> had been besieged, but this was the first time the citadel of Sardis had fallen and an enemy force could march on to Halicarnassus without significant obstacles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> were forced onto the defensive in the cities they still held.</p><p>Alexander commemorated the victory. The inscription over the armour sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> read, "Alexander, son of Philip and all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> except the Lacedaemonians, present this offering from the spoils taken from the barbarians inhabiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>", which marked the absence of the Spartans from his united Greek army. At Dion, in the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, he erected the Granicus Monument, bronze statues by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysippus">Lysippus</a> of himself and the twenty-five companions who had died in the first cavalry charge, all on horseback. In 146 BCE, after the Fourth Macedonian War, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-macedonicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Macedonicus</a> took the group to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where it was displayed in a portico he built below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a>.</p><p>The site of the battle has recently been made a protected historic site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In May 334 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> defeated the army of the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> at the crossing of the Granicus. The Persian losses are put at 5,000 to 6,000 by one modern estimate, and the satraps were left on the defensive as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and other cities fell.</em></p>

